course: dictionary
lesson: 13238
title: The genitive: showing who owns something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The genitive is the form of a noun or pronoun that shows who owns something or what it belongs to. In English you usually meet it as the possessive apostrophe. «The dog's bone» means the bone that belongs to the dog. «Sam's coat» means the coat that belongs to Sam.
explain: Look at the clue words. The owner comes first, then the thing owned. Ask yourself: who has this? If you can answer that, you have found the genitive. English also shows it with «of», as in «the roof of the house».
explain: Foreign-language lessons use the grammar word genitive. In languages such as Latin, German, Ukrainian and Russian, the ending of the word changes to show ownership, and that changed ending is called the genitive case. The same idea can also mean «of». So when you meet the word genitive in a language lesson, you already know what it is doing.
fact: Genitive is a grammar word for the form of a noun that shows ownership.
fact: In English, the genitive is often called the possessive.
fact: «Sam's coat» is a genitive: the coat belongs to Sam.
fact: In Latin, German, Ukrainian and Russian, the genitive is a case: the ending of the word changes.
fact: In languages with a genitive case, the genitive ending can also mean «of».
exercise: «The cat's bowl is empty.» Which word is the genitive? | is | empty | bowl | *cat's
exercise: «Mia's bike is red.» What does the genitive show here? | When the bike was bought | What colour the bike is | Where the bike is | *Who owns the bike
exercise: Which phrase shows the genitive? | Sam is cold | Run quickly home | A tall, green tree | *Sam's coat
test: «The dog's bone was buried under the tree.» What does the genitive show? | Where the tree is | When it was buried | *Who the bone belongs to | How big the bone is
test: Why might a language teacher use the word genitive instead of possessive? | Genitive means happy | Genitive is a kind of fruit | English has no possessives at all | *Genitive is the grammar word used in foreign-language lessons, such as Latin and German
exam: «In Latin and German, the genitive ending shows ownership and can also mean "of".» What can the genitive ending do in those languages? | *Show ownership and mean "of" | Make a noun plural only | Turn a sentence into a question | Only count numbers
